     "title": "Two Managers, One Rulebook: A Service Company Case",
     "content": "Picture a home-repair company running two crews under two different managers, Priya and Doug. Before any of this systemization, Priya tends to waive the standard trip charge whenever a customer complains about wait time, because she personally dislikes conflict; Doug never waives it, because nobody ever told him he could, and he assumes head office would object. Neither manager is doing anything wrong exactly, but the company is effectively running two different refund policies depending purely on which manager happens to answer the phone, and the owner has no visibility into either pattern because neither decision was ever written down anywhere. When the owner sits down to build the Operations Manual's management section, the real work isn't drafting new rules from scratch so much as surfacing the rule that should have existed the whole time: under what conditions is a trip charge waived, who decides, and what gets logged. Interviewing both managers turns up a genuinely useful distinction neither had previously articulated - Priya's waivers cluster around cases where the technician arrived more than 30 minutes late, while Doug's non-waivers include several cases that, on reflection, he agrees should have been waived under the same logic. The written rule that emerges, waive automatically if arrival is more than 30 minutes past the scheduled window, log every waiver either way, escalate anything outside that condition to the owner, isn't just fair to customers; it also lets the owner audit, for the first time, how often trip charges are actually being waived company-wide and why - a pattern that was previously invisible because it lived separately in two people's private judgment rather than in one shared, written system.",
