     "title": "Exercise: Stress-Test Your Own Venture Against Both Failure Modes",
     "content": "Apply this lesson's critical lens to your own business idea, project, or role rather than to a hypothetical case study. First, check for Founder One's risk: have you, or has your organization, built more process, documentation, or standardization around a product or offer than the current evidence justifies, polished operating procedures for something that hasn't yet been tested against real customer behavior at any scale? If so, name one assumption about the core offer that's still unvalidated, and describe the cheapest, fastest real-world test you could run this month to check it, before investing further in systemizing it. Second, check for Founder Two's risk: have you validated that people want what you're offering, but you're still running it in a way that depends entirely on your personal memory and daily improvisation, no written positions, no documented decision rules, no defined customer profile guiding operations? If so, this is the moment this book's earlier lessons, Organizational Strategy, Management Strategy, People Strategy, Marketing Strategy, become directly useful, rather than premature. Finally, write two or three sentences stating where your venture currently sits on the spectrum between needs more validation before it deserves more systemization and is validated enough that under-systemizing it is now the bigger risk. There's no universally correct answer - the point of this closing exercise is to make that judgment explicit and revisit it deliberately as the business changes, rather than defaulting permanently to whichever instinct, system-building or improvising, feels most natural to you personally.",
